// Procedure — the two-step develop-and-read protocol. Content markup only.

= Procedure

To a sample containing between 5 and $100 #h(0.1em) mu"g"$ of protein in
$0.2 #h(0.1em) "ml"$, add $1.0 #h(0.1em) "ml"$ of reagent C, mix at once, and
let stand at room temperature for ten minutes or longer. The copper--protein
complex forms during this interval.

Then add $0.10 #h(0.1em) "ml"$ of the phenol reagent _very rapidly_ and with
_immediate_ mixing — this is the one critical manipulation. The reagent is
reduced only while the medium remains alkaline, and it is destroyed by the alkali
within seconds; unless it is dispersed before it is spent, the colour falls short
and runs low. After thirty minutes, during which the blue develops to its full
depth, read the absorbance against a reagent blank.

The wavelength is chosen to suit the protein concentration. Near $750 #h(0.1em)
"m" mu$ the chromogen absorbs most strongly and the method is at its most
sensitive; for the more concentrated samples the weaker band at $500 #h(0.1em)
"m" mu$ keeps the reading on scale. A single absorbance value, referred to the
standard curve run in parallel, gives the protein content directly.
